CertHub.register({"id":"project-plus","vendor":"CompTIA","name":"CompTIA Project+","short":"Project+","exam":"PK0-005","blurb":"Entry-level IT project management: core concepts, the project life cycle, tools and documentation, and the IT and governance basics a project coordinator or junior project manager needs, including agile.","status":"check","statusNote":"CompTIA's pages could not be opened from this environment. The four domains and weights (Project management concepts 33%, Project life cycle phases 30%, Tools and documentation 19%, Basics of IT and governance 18%) and the exam facts come from the published PK0-005 exam guide as best known. Confirm them, the passing score and whether a newer Project+ version has been announced on the official CompTIA Project+ page before booking.","lastVerified":"2026-09-25","notices":[],"examInfo":{"questions":"Maximum of 90 (multiple choice and performance-based)","minutes":90,"pass":"710 on a 100–900 scale (confirm on the official page)","extra":"No prerequisites are required. CompTIA suggests some experience managing or coordinating small to medium IT projects. Project+ does not require continuing education renewal; check the current policy on the official page."},"examSim":{"questions":90,"minutes":90},"sources":[{"label":"CompTIA Project+ certification and PK0-005 exam objectives","url":"https://www.comptia.org/certifications/project"}],"planWeeks":8,"hoursPerWeek":"5–7","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Project management concepts","w":33,"topics":["Project basics: projects vs operations and programs, the triple constraint, and project roles (sponsor, project manager, PMO, team, stakeholders)","Change control process: change requests, impact assessment, change control board, approval, implementation and communicating changes","Types of change: scope creep, gold plating, timeline, budget, resource, requirements and emergency changes","Risk management activities: identification, qualitative and quantitative analysis, risk register, probability and impact matrix","Risk responses: avoid, mitigate, transfer, accept, escalate, exploit, enhance and share; contingency and management reserves","Communication management: communication plan, synchronous vs asynchronous methods, audience, frequency and tailoring","Stakeholder management: stakeholder register, power/interest grid, engagement and managing expectations","Resource management: resource types, allocation, leveling vs smoothing, shared resources and RACI charts","Schedule development: activity sequencing, dependencies (FS, SS, FF, SF), critical path, float, milestones, crashing and fast-tracking","Agile methodology: Scrum roles, events and artifacts, user stories, backlog refinement, velocity and Kanban","Other methodologies: waterfall (predictive), hybrid, PRINCE2, Lean and Six Sigma (DMAIC), and choosing an approach","Team dynamics and conflict resolution: team development stages, conflict techniques, motivation and running effective meetings"],"notes":["Exam objectives domain 1.0 (confirm objective numbering in the official PK0-005 objectives)"],"labs":["In a free spreadsheet (LibreOffice Calc or Google Sheets), build a risk register for a small office network upgrade: ten risks with probability, impact, score, owner and response type, then color it as a probability and impact heat map.","Draw a six-task network diagram on paper or in draw.io (free, no account needed), calculate early and late start and finish by hand, and mark the critical path and each task's float.","Run a one-week personal Scrum: write six user stories for a home project, estimate them in story points, plan a sprint on a free Trello board (stay on the free plan and delete the board when done), hold a short daily check-in and a written retrospective."]},{"id":2,"name":"Project life cycle phases","w":30,"topics":["Discovery and concept preparation: business case, feasibility, ROI and payback period, and preliminary scope","Initiating: project charter, sponsor authorization, high-level scope, assumptions, constraints and success criteria","Initiating: identifying stakeholders, assigning the project manager, and holding the kickoff meeting","Planning: requirements gathering, scope statement, work breakdown structure (WBS) and WBS dictionary","Planning: estimating time and cost (analogous, parametric, three-point/PERT, bottom-up) and setting baselines","Planning: budget development and procurement (make vs buy, RFI, RFP, RFQ, statement of work, contract types)","Planning: subsidiary plans for quality, risk, communication, transition and release","Executing: directing the work, managing vendors, quality assurance vs quality control, and deliverable acceptance","Monitoring and controlling: tracking progress against baselines, variance analysis, issue management and escalation","Closing: formal acceptance, transition to operations, contract closure, releasing resources, lessons learned and archiving"],"notes":["Exam objectives domain 2.0: discovery/concept preparation, initiating, planning, executing and closing phases"],"labs":["Write a one-page project charter for a real or imagined IT project (for example, moving a small office to a new file server): purpose, objectives, high-level scope, milestones, budget estimate, assumptions, constraints, sponsor and success criteria.","Build a three-level WBS for the same project in draw.io or a free mind-map tool, then write WBS dictionary entries (description, owner, acceptance criteria, estimate) for three work packages.","Enter the WBS into ProjectLibre or GanttProject (both free, open-source desktop tools), add durations and dependencies, save a baseline, then change two actual durations and read the variance the tool reports."]},{"id":3,"name":"Tools and documentation","w":19,"topics":["Project management software: scheduling tools, Gantt charts, Kanban boards, ticketing systems and dashboards","Collaboration tools: real-time vs asynchronous communication, shared workspaces, file sharing and document version control","Quality charts: Pareto, histogram, run and control charts, scatter diagrams and fishbone (Ishikawa) diagrams","Agile reporting: burndown and burnup charts, velocity charts and cumulative flow diagrams","Earned value management: PV, EV, AC, CV, SV, CPI, SPI, EAC and ETC, and interpreting the results","Core project documents: project management plan, schedule, RACI, risk register, issue log, change log and assumption log","Meeting and status documentation: agendas, minutes, action items, status reports and executive summaries","Vendor and procurement documents: NDA, MOU, SLA, SOW, purchase orders, invoices and change orders","Decision-making tools: SWOT, cost-benefit analysis, decision trees and expected monetary value, brainstorming and root cause analysis"],"notes":["Exam objectives domain 3.0"],"labs":["In a free spreadsheet, record 40 made-up help desk tickets by category, sort the counts and build a Pareto chart with a cumulative percentage line; decide which two categories a project should tackle first.","Create an earned value calculator in a spreadsheet: enter BAC, PV, EV and AC for three reporting periods and compute CV, SV, CPI, SPI, EAC and ETC; write one sentence interpreting each period.","Set up a free Kanban board (Trello, GitHub Projects or a paper wall) with To do, In progress (WIP limit 3), Review and Done columns; move ten cards through it over a week and sketch the resulting burnup chart. If you use an online service, stay on the free plan and delete the board afterward."]},{"id":4,"name":"Basics of IT and governance","w":18,"topics":["Security concepts for IT projects: CIA triad, least privilege, access reviews and building security requirements in from the start","Data privacy and compliance: PII, PHI, data classification, retention, GDPR, HIPAA, PCI DSS and audits","IT infrastructure basics: servers, networks, storage and endpoints, and how infrastructure changes affect projects","Cloud and deployment models: on-premises, IaaS, PaaS, SaaS, public, private and hybrid cloud, and their trade-offs","Software development life cycle and DevOps: CI/CD, development, test, staging and production environments, and release management","Operational change management: change advisory board, maintenance windows, change freezes, rollback plans and downtime","Physical and environmental considerations: facility access, power and cooling, asset tracking and secure disposal","Governance: PMO standards, phase gate reviews, organizational change management, training and user adoption","Business continuity for IT projects: backups, disaster recovery, RTO and RPO, and testing before go-live"],"notes":["Exam objectives domain 4.0"],"labs":["Write an operational change request for a server patching window: description, business justification, risk and impact, affected users, maintenance window, test plan, rollback plan and approvers. Compare it with the change fields in a free ticketing tool's template.","Classify ten sample data items (customer email, card number, patient diagnosis, public price list and so on) as public, internal, confidential or regulated, and note which rule (GDPR, HIPAA, PCI DSS) would apply.","Use a cloud provider's free pricing calculator (no account needed) to compare the monthly cost of a small VM with a SaaS alternative for the same need; if you create a free-tier account to explore, set a budget alert and delete every resource afterward."]}],"study":{"1":[["What makes a project different from operations?","A project is temporary, with a defined start and end, and produces a unique product, service or result. Operations are ongoing and repetitive, such as running the help desk. A program is a group of related projects managed together for shared benefits."],["Walk through the change control process.","Someone submits a change request; the PM logs it and assesses its impact on scope, schedule, cost, quality and risk; the change control board (or the authority named in the plan) approves or rejects it; approved changes are implemented, baselines and documents are updated, and the decision is communicated to stakeholders."],["What is the difference between a risk and an issue?","A risk is an uncertain future event that could affect the project, recorded in the risk register with probability, impact and a planned response. An issue is something happening now that must be resolved, tracked in the issue log with an owner and a due date."],["Name the four negative-risk responses and give an example of each.","Avoid: change the plan to remove the risk (skip an unproven product). Mitigate: reduce probability or impact (rehearse a migration). Transfer: shift it to a third party (insurance, fixed-price contract). Accept: take no action or set a contingency reserve for low-priority risks."],["How do resource leveling and resource smoothing differ?","Leveling resolves over-allocation and can move the end date. Smoothing adjusts assignments only within available float, so the end date does not change, but it may not remove every conflict."],["What are the three Scrum roles and what does each do?","The product owner owns and orders the product backlog to maximize value. The scrum master coaches the team in Scrum and removes impediments. The developers build a usable increment each sprint and manage their own work."]],"2":[["What goes into a project charter, and why does it matter?","Purpose and objectives, high-level scope and requirements, milestones, summary budget, key stakeholders, assumptions, constraints, success criteria and the named PM with their authority. Signed by the sponsor, it formally authorizes the project."],["Compare analogous, parametric, three-point and bottom-up estimating.","Analogous uses a similar past project (fast, less accurate). Parametric multiplies a unit rate by quantity. Three-point (PERT) uses optimistic, most likely and pessimistic values, (O + 4M + P) / 6. Bottom-up sums estimates for each work package (slowest, most accurate)."],["When would you use a fixed-price contract versus time and materials?","Fixed price suits a well-defined scope: the seller carries the cost risk. Time and materials suits uncertain or short-term work such as staff augmentation: the buyer pays for hours and materials and carries more risk."],["What is the difference between quality assurance and quality control?","Quality assurance checks that the right processes are followed to prevent defects (audits, process reviews). Quality control inspects and tests the deliverables themselves to find defects."],["List the main closing activities.","Obtain formal acceptance, hand off to operations with documentation and training, close contracts and pay final invoices, reconcile the budget, release resources, hold lessons learned, archive project records and communicate closure."]],"3":[["Which chart would you use to find the biggest sources of defects, and why?","A Pareto chart, which sorts categories by frequency with a cumulative line, so the few causes behind most problems (the 80/20 idea) stand out."],["Define CPI and SPI and explain how to read them.","CPI = EV / AC; SPI = EV / PV. A value above 1 is good (under budget or ahead of schedule), exactly 1 is on plan, and below 1 means over budget or behind schedule."],["How does a burnup chart differ from a burndown chart?","A burndown shows work remaining falling toward zero. A burnup shows work completed rising toward a separate total-scope line, so scope changes are visible as that line moves."],["What should meeting minutes contain?","Date, attendees, topics discussed, decisions made and action items, each with an owner and due date. They are distributed promptly so everyone has the same record."],["What is the difference between an SLA, an SOW and an NDA?","An SLA sets measurable service levels such as uptime and response time. An SOW describes the work, deliverables and timeline a vendor will provide. An NDA legally protects confidential information shared between parties."]],"4":[["Why must an IT project consider data privacy from the start?","Personal and regulated data (PII, PHI, card data) brings legal obligations on how it is collected, stored, shared and deleted. Adding controls late is expensive and can delay go-live or cause fines."],["What should a production change request include?","Description and justification, risk and impact analysis, affected systems and users, implementation steps, test plan, schedule or maintenance window, rollback (backout) plan, communication plan and approvals from the change advisory board."],["Compare IaaS, PaaS and SaaS from a project manager's point of view.","IaaS: the team manages the OS and everything above it on rented infrastructure. PaaS: the team manages only its code and data. SaaS: the team configures and adopts a finished application, so the work shifts to integration, data migration and training."],["Explain RTO and RPO.","The recovery time objective is how long a service can be down before it must be restored. The recovery point objective is how much data, measured in time, can be lost; it drives backup frequency."],["Why do organizations use change freezes?","To protect stability during critical business periods such as year-end close or peak sales. Only emergency changes are allowed, so project deployments must be planned around the freeze."]]},"qCount":90,"hasLessons":true,"hasPbqs":true,"hasQuestionsEs":true});
